/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522681 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522681 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186522548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522681 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f4f7d0a9-5a27-5459-8a96-37790d16d10f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522685 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186522194 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522685 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186522261 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522685 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186522390 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522685 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186522469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522685 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186522503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522685 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522685 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522685 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186522503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522685 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fa4911c0-8b9b-5ded-8541-7a649e780685" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522687 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186522153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522687 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186522198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522687 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186522230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522687 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186522393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522687 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186522502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522687 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522687 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522687 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186522393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522687 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fd74e72c-2c1f-53f8-8056-3995854a281c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522696 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522664 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522696 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105982" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522696 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522696 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $P(F|G)=0.5,$ $P(F)=0.25,$ and $P(G)=0.3,$ compute $P(G|F).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522696 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "062302ea-c279-555c-ae6b-c50720d77be2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522696 #edb/ref 1074 "Using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(G|F) & =\\frac{P(F|G)P(G)}{P(F)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.5)(0.3)}{0.25} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.15}{0.25} \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522700 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522700 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106242" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522700 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522700 #edb/ref 1071 "A survey of adult citizens of a particular city showed that $32%$ of all females and $5%$ of all males don't eat after $6$ p.m. $51%$ of the population are female. If a person who eats after $6$ p.m. is selected at random, what is the probability of them being male? Express your answer as a percentage to the nearest percent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522700 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0ba278eb-bd8e-5c0e-8df1-e6e393201d18" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522700 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $E$ be the event that a randomly selected person eats after $6$ p.m., and let $M$ be the event that they are male. Then, we require $P(M|E),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(M|E)=\\frac{P(E|M)P(M)}{P(E)}=\\frac{P(E|M)P(M)}{P(E|M)P(M)+P(E|{M}^{′})P({M}^{′})}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since $32%$ of all females and  $5%$ of all males don't eat after $6$ p.m, we have\n\n$$\nP(E|M)=1-0.05=0.95,\\,P(E|{M}^{′})=1-0.32=0.68.\n$$\n\n- Since $51%$ of the population are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(M)=1-0.51=0.49,\\,P({M}^{′})=0.51.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(M|E) & =\\frac{P(E|M)P(M)}{P(E|M)P(M)+P(E|{M}^{′})P({M}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.95)(0.49)}{(0.95)(0.49)+(0.68)(0.51)} \\\\  & ≈57%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest percent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522727 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522604 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522727 #edb/ref 1069 "q-42569" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522727 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522727 #edb/ref 1071 "On a particular industrial estate, the probability of smoke on a given day is $10%$ and the probability of a dangerous fire is $1.5%.$ It is known that $90%$ of dangerous fires create smoke. What is the probability of a dangerous fire when there is smoke? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522727 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4fe71dad-9ede-5ba8-b760-d7b3b5dd3ac6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522727 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $S$ be the event that there is smoke, and let $F$ be the event that there is a dangerous fire. Then, we require $P(F|S),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(F|S)=\\frac{P(S|F)P(F)}{P(S)}\n$$\n\n- $P(S)=0.1,$ since the probability of smoke is $10%.$\n\n- $P(F)=0.015,$ since the probability of dangerous fire is $1.5%.$\n\n- $P(S|F)=0.9,$ since  $90%$ of dangerous fires make smoke.\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|S) & =\\frac{P(S|F)P(F)}{P(S)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.9\\cdot 0.015}{0.1} \\\\  & ≈0.14,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522738 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522674 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522738 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106498" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522738 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522738 #edb/ref 1071 "A laboratory test for a particular disease is conducted on some patients. Of the tested patients, $94%$ of those infected and $7%$ of those not infected tested positive for the disease. Only $12%$ of the entire population is infected with the disease. What is the probability that a randomly chosen patient that tested negative is not infected? Round your answer to four decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522738 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "729d63e1-69ab-5f12-b423-6126fc288807" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522738 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $D$ be the event that a randomly chosen patient is infected with the disease, and let $T$ be the event they tested positive. Then, we require $P({D}^{′}|{T}^{′}),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP({D}^{′}|{T}^{′})=\\frac{P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}{P({T}^{′})}=\\frac{P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}{P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})+P({T}^{′}|D)P(D)}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since the probability of an infected person testing positive is $94%,$ and the probability of a non-infected person testing positive is $7%,$ we have\n\n$$\nP({T}^{′}|D)=1-0.94=0.06,\\,P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})=1-0.07=0.93.\n$$\n\n- Since only $12%$ of the population has the disease, we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=0.12\\,P({D}^{′})=1-0.12=0.88.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({D}^{′}|{T}^{′}) & =\\frac{P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}{P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})+P({T}^{′}|D)P(D)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.93)(0.88)}{(0.93)(0.88)+(0.06)(0.12)} \\\\  & ≈0.9913\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to four decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522740 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522740 #edb/ref 1069 "q-170678" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522740 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522740 #edb/ref 1071 "At a soccer game, $60%$ of the spectators are men. It is known that $20%$ of the men in the crowd are over $50,$ while $30%$ of all people in the crowd are over $50.$ If a person over $50$ is picked at random from the crowd, what is the probability that the person will be a man?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522740 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "74a09e82-c6f4-5ba5-932c-01b4112c68fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522740 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $M$ be the event that the randomly selected person is a man, and let $F$ be the event that it is over $50.$ Then, we require $P(M|F),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(M|F)=\\frac{P(F|M)P(M)}{P(F)}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- $P(F|M)=0.2,$ since $20%$ of the men at the game are over $50.$\n\n- $P(M)=0.6,$ since $60%$ of all the spectators are men.\n\n- $P(F)=0.3,$ since $30%$ of all spectators are over $50.$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(M|F) & =\\frac{P(F|M)P(M)}{P(F)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.2)(0.6)}{0.3} \\\\  & =0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522742 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522742 #edb/ref 1069 "q-170679" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522742 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522742 #edb/ref 1071 "On an MLB team, $75%$ of all players are American citizens. It is known that $15%$ of them also speak Spanish, while $25%$ of all players on the team speak Spanish. If a player who speaks Spanish is randomly selected for a TV interview, what is the probability that he is American? Express your answer as a percentage." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522742 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7838b043-50f3-55a8-b1f4-349f8ab97093" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522742 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $A$ be the event that the randomly selected player is American, and let $S$ be the event that a player speaks Spanish. Then, we require $P(A|S),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(A|S)=\\frac{P(S|A)P(A)}{P(S)}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- $P(S|A)=0.15,$ since $15%$ of all American players on the team speak spanish.\n\n- $P(A)=0.75,$ since $0.75%$ of the players on the team are American citizens.\n\n- $P(S)=0.25,$ since  $25%$ of all players on the team speak Spanish.\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A|S) & =\\frac{P(S|A)P(A)}{P(S)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.15)(0.75)}{0.25} \\\\  & =0.45.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522752 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522685 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522752 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105987" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522752 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522752 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $P(S|T)=0.3,$ $P(S)=0.6,$ and $P(T)=0.5,$ compute $P(T|S).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522752 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "884aeeae-3d24-5d63-bf46-362bb4b6d4a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522752 #edb/ref 1074 "Using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(T|S) & =\\frac{P(S|T)P(T)}{P(S)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.3)(0.5)}{0.6} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.15}{0.6} \\\\  & =0.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522759 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522609 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522759 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105986" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522759 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522759 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $P(L|K)=0.6,$ $P(K)=0.4,$ and $P(L)=0.5,$ compute $P(K|L).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522759 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "92bd4292-ffa2-5865-9aeb-f65630d4d79e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522759 #edb/ref 1074 "Using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(K|L) & =\\frac{P(L|K)P(K)}{P(L)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.6)(0.4)}{0.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.24}{0.5} \\\\  & =0.48.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522778 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522778 #edb/ref 1069 "q-5788" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522778 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522778 #edb/ref 1071 "A laboratory test for a particular allergy is conducted on some patients. Of the tested patients, $96%$ of allergy sufferers and $3%$ of non-allergic patients tested positive for the allergy. Only $4%$ of the entire population has the allergy. What is the probability that a randomly chosen patient that tested positive is an allergy sufferer? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522778 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "befc2ac4-e448-5384-bd13-1313e616793b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522778 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $A$ be the event that a randomly chosen patient is an allergy sufferer, and let $T$ be the event they tested positive. Then, we require $P(A|T),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(A|T)=\\frac{P(T|A)P(A)}{P(T)}=\\frac{P(T|A)P(A)}{P(T|A)P(A)+P(T|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since the probability of a person who has the allergy testing positive is $96%,$ and the probability of a non-allergic person testing positive is $3%,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(T|A)=0.96,\\,P(T|{A}^{′})=0.03.\n$$\n\n- Since only $4%$ of the population suffers the allergy, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.04,\\,P({A}^{′})=1-0.04=0.96.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A|T) & =\\frac{P(T|A)P(A)}{P(T|A)P(A)+P(T|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.96)(0.04)}{(0.96)(0.04)+(0.03)(0.96)} \\\\  & ≈0.57\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522784 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522784 #edb/ref 1069 "q-5787" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522784 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522784 #edb/ref 1071 "A laboratory test for a particular disease is conducted on some patients. Of the tested patients, $95%$ of those infected and $4%$ of those not infected tested positive for the disease. Only $10%$ of the entire population is infected with the disease. What is the probability that a randomly chosen patient that tested positive is infected? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522784 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d0b4bd1c-2214-5d30-8eda-6058d4c56287" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522784 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $D$ be the event that a randomly chosen patient is infected with the disease, and let $T$ be the event they tested positive. Then, we require $P(D|T),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(D|T)=\\frac{P(T|D)P(D)}{P(T)}=\\frac{P(T|D)P(D)}{P(T|D)P(D)+P(T|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since the probability of an infected person testing positive is $95%,$ and the probability of a non-infected person testing positive is $4%,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(T|D)=0.95,\\,P(T|{D}^{′})=0.04.\n$$\n\n- Since only $10%$ of the population has the disease, we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=0.1,\\,P({D}^{′})=1-0.1=0.9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D|T) & =\\frac{P(T|D)P(D)}{P(T|D)P(D)+P(T|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.95)(0.1)}{(0.95)(0.1)+(0.04)(0.9)} \\\\  & ≈0.73\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522790 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522790 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106224" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522790 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522790 #edb/ref 1071 "A survey of Bulgarian citizens showed that $17%$ of all females and  $38%$ of all males smoke. $51%$ of the population are female. If a smoker from this population is selected at random, what is the probability of them being female? Express your answer as a percentage to the nearest percent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522790 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d7bc91a9-28bf-5e94-8b54-f2a8632dbd6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522790 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $S$ be the event that a randomly selected person smokes, and let $F$ be the event that they are female. Then, we require $P(F|S),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(F|S)=\\frac{P(S|F)P(F)}{P(S)}=\\frac{P(S|F)P(F)}{P(S|F)P(F)+P(S|{F}^{′})P({F}^{′})}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since $17%$ of all females and  $38%$ of all males smoke, we have\n\n$$\nP(S|F)=0.17,\\,P(S|{F}^{′})=0.38.\n$$\n\n- Since $51%$ of the population are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.51,\\,P({F}^{′})=1-0.51=0.49.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|S) & =\\frac{P(S|F)P(F)}{P(S|F)P(F)+P(S|{F}^{′})P({F}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.17)(0.51)}{(0.17)(0.51)+(0.38)(0.49)} \\\\  & ≈32%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest percent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522799 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522799 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105984" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522799 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522799 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $P(M|L)=0.15,$ $P(M)=0.35,$ and $P(L)=0.14,$ compute $P(L|M).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522799 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e9b91c35-32fc-5eb1-b159-6a8e41b29e96" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522799 #edb/ref 1074 "Using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(L|M) & =\\frac{P(M|L)P(L)}{P(M)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.15)(0.14)}{0.35} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.021}{0.35} \\\\  & =0.06.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522807 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522807 #edb/ref 1069 "q-170681" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522807 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522807 #edb/ref 1071 "Two groups of high school students are competing in a math contest. Group $A$ consists of $5$ girls and $3$ boys, while Group $B$ consists of $2$ girls and $4$ boys. For the first challenge, one group is randomly selected, and one student is randomly selected from that group. If the selected student is a girl, what is the probability that she is from Group $B?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522807 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f5454beb-e917-5a8e-86e1-4e0232e2e094" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186522807 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $B$ be the event that Group $B$ is selected, and let $G$ be the event that a randomly selected student is a girl. Then, we require $P(B|G),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(B|G)=\\frac{P(G|B)P(B)}{P(G)}=\\frac{P(G|B)P(B)}{P(G|B)P(B)+P(G|{B}^{′})P({B}^{′})}\n$$\n\nNote that ${B}^{′}$ is the event that Group A is selected.\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since Group $A$ consists of $5$ girls and $3$ boys and Group $B$ consists of $2$ girls and $4$ boys, we have\n\n$$\nP(G|B)=\\frac{2}{6}=\\frac{1}{3},\\,P(G|{B}^{′})=\\frac{5}{8}.\n$$\n\n- Since the group is selected at random, we have\n\n$$\nP(B)=P({B}^{′})=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B|G) & =\\frac{P(G|B)P(B)}{P(G|B)P(B)+P(G|{B}^{′})P({B}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{5}{8}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{6})}{(\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{5}{16})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{6})}{(\\frac{23}{48})} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{23}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
